{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 46, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent Crusader nobleman and one of the most notable rulers of the County of Tripoli during the Middle Ages. Born around 1140 he was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem making him a member of the prestigious House of Toulouse. His life and reign are marked by significant military and political achievements in the Crusader states especially during the tumultuous period following the fall of the County of Edessa to Zengi in 1144.\n\nUpon the death of his father in 1152 Raymond III inherited the County of Tripoli. His early years as ruler were challenging due to the instability in the region and the ongoing conflicts with Muslim forces. He was just a child when he succeeded his father forcing his mother Hodierna to act as regent until he came of age. During this time the Crusader states were under immense pressure from Muslim counterattacks and internal strife. Raymond III’s reign would later see him become a key player in the defense of the Crusader states.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Military and Political Career**\nRaymond’s military prowess became evident early in his reign. By the time he reached adulthood he had established himself as a capable and respected leader. He played a crucial role in the defense of the Crusader states particularly during the siege of Harim in 1157 where he successfully repelled Muslim forces. His alliances with other Crusader leaders such as Baldwin III of Jerusalem and Amalric I were instrumental in maintaining stability in the region.\n\nRaymond’s diplomatic skills were also noteworthy. He navigated complex relationships with both Christian and Muslim rulers often forging temporary truces to secure his territory. His marriage to Eschiva of Bures in 1164 solidified his alliances with other important noble families in the Crusader states. Despite these efforts the region remained under constant threat from the expanding Ayyubid dynasty led by Saladin.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Conflicts with Saladin and the Fall of Jerusalem**\nThe rise of Saladin posed one of the greatest challenges to Raymond’s rule. As Saladin’s power grew in the late 1160s and early 1170s he began to encroach on Crusader territories. Raymond found himself in a precarious position forced to balance alliances and military campaigns to protect Tripoli. His efforts were often hampered by internal divisions within the Crusader states.\n\nOne of the most critical moments in Raymond’s career came during the Battle of La Forbie in 1179. Though he was not present at the battle its aftermath highlighted the vulnerability of the Crusader states. The defeat dealt a severe blow to the Christian forces and accelerated the decline of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond’s response was to strengthen Tripoli’s defenses and prepare for further conflicts.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Later Years Death and Legacy**\nIn the later years of his life Raymond III continued to defend Tripoli against Saladin’s forces. His reign was characterized by resilience and a determination to preserve the Crusader states despite overwhelming odds. Raymond died in 1187 possibly from dysentery at the age of 47. His death coincided with the fall of Jerusalem to Saladin later that year marking the beginning of the end for the Crusader states.\n\nRaymond’s legacy endures as one of the most steadfast defenders of the Crusader cause. He is remembered for his military leadership diplomatic acumen and unwavering commitment to preserving Christian rule in the Holy Land. His county of Tripoli remained a bastion of Christian resistance for decades after his death though it eventually fell to the Mamluks in 1289. Historians often credit Raymond III with buying crucial time for the Crusader states allowing them to endure through periods of intense pressure.", "logprobs": [{"token": "R